  "query_preview": "outlook for all three locations (36 hours to day 7):\nForecast guidance and pattern recognition all points to at least \nscattered shower potential continuing into next weekend, with a few \nthunderstorms mixed in from time to time. A tropical \ndisturbance/circulation located east of Pohnpei/northeast of Kosrae \nwill not only maintain respectable convergence in the cyclonically- \nenhanced monsoon flow, but there may be a few periods of locally \nincreased shower coverage and intensity.\n\nMarine and surf conditions:\nA west to southwest primary and secondary swell will continue through\nthe weekend at Pohnpei and Kosrae, with a slight backing of swell\ndirection due to the previously-mentioned cyclonic flow in the\nmonsoon environment, will allow for a southwest and south swell\ndirection to continue at Majuro. This swell regime will continue to\nbe augmented by the persistent cross-equatorial flow/swell pattern.\nThe net result is elevated surf concerns will continue the next\nseveral days at Kosrae and Majuro as well. Otherwise, mainly gentle\nto light winds currently, may become light to gentle from time to\ntime, especially for the latter half of next week. Seas of 5 to 7\nfeet are anticipated through the forecast period.\n\nWestern Micronesia...\nAs Typhoon Saudel well northwest of the Marianas and Tropical \nDepression 19W north-northwest of Yap continue to move away from the\nregion, the last pulse of a fresh to strong southwesterly monsoon is\nexpected to bring more patchy showers Yap tonight. While winds are \nexpected to diminish slightly thereafter, a reigniting monsoon surge \nis still expected to pummel Yap and much of eastern Yap State the \nnext several days, with a peak shower and thunder coverage expected \nTuesday and Tuesday night. A tropical ridge anchored just north of \nthe Equator is expected to bring a dry spell to Palau beginning \nMonday night, which is likely to last for the rest of the week. While\nmoist westerlies are expected to continue at Chuuk for most of the \nforecast period, the aforementioned tropical ridge will allow Chuuk \nto fall in between the convectively active pattern hugging 10N and \nthe much quieter sector of the Equator between Indonesia and \nKapingamarangi. This will allow patchy showers and some thunderstorms\nto form at Chuuk for much of the forecast period, followed by a \ndrying trend in the second half of next week as a larger buffer \ncirculation induces more diffusive flow.\n\nThe aforementioned monsoon pulse and remaining significant\nsouthwesterly swells and fresh to strong winds continues to produce\nsea conditions hazardous to small craft, which are expected to fall\nbelow hazardous criteria by sunrise on Monday. In the meantime,\ncombined seas of 5 to 7 feet in Palau waters are expected to taper\ndown a few feet over the next few days as the southwesterly swell\ndiminishes, being slowly replenished by various smaller northerly\nswells building in. Combined seas of 5 to 7 feet are expected to\nslowly drop a foot or two toward the next weekend as swells and\nlocalized wind waves diminish.",
